{"id":["contract.json","/premises"],"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"contract.json\", \"pointer\": \"/premises\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": [{\"id\": \"H001\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"E is nonempty.\", \"type\": \"E.Nonempty\"}, {\"id\": \"H002\", \"kind\": \"hypothesis\", \"statement\": \"E is compact in Euclidean ℝ^n₀.\", \"type\": \"IsCompact E\"}, {\"id\": \"E001\", \"kind\": \"external\", \"statement\": \"For every n≥0 and every S⊆Rn(n)×ℝ equipped with the maximum product norm, euclideanLift n sends S to an equal-dimensional set in WithLp 2 (Rn(n)×ℝ). This is the identity on underlying pairs and equips the target with the Euclidean product norm.\", \"source\": {\"statement\": \"For every n≥0 and every S⊆Rn(n)×ℝ equipped with the maximum product norm, euclideanLift n sends S to an equal-dimensional set in WithLp 2 (Rn(n)×ℝ). This is the identity on underlying pairs and equips the target with the Euclidean product norm.\", \"hypotheses\": [], \"author\": \"Mathlib contributors; supplied 2basis author for the prepared B011 adapter\", \"title\": \"Prepared basis B011: maximum-product to L²-product Hausdorff dimension adapter\", \"version\": \"Frozen basis-registry.json, pinned Mathlib 905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"identifier\": \"basis-registry.json B011; provisional port E001\", \"locator\": \"basis-registry.json /dependencies/10, /lean_header; Mathlib/Analysis/Normed/Lp/ProdLp.lean:546–562; Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean:412–420\", \"notation_map\": \"Rn(n)=EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n); ordinary pair norm is maximum; WithLp 2 uses the L² product norm; euclideanLift n is WithLp.toLp 2 and equals the inverse of prodContinuousLinearEquiv. The source's n and S are universally quantified dummy variables.\", \"errata\": \"No conflicting correction is present in the frozen basis. The adapter was compared with the currently pinned source, rather than inferred from an unrelated edition.\", \"source_kind\": \"prepared class-1 adapted basis theorem with closed proof in the pinned environment\", \"origin\": \"B011 is the sole authorized provisional E port in the frozen 2basis registry; B101–B105 are proved internally here.\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-05\", \"source_statement\": \"For every n≥0 and every S⊆Rn(n)×ℝ equipped with the maximum product norm, euclideanLift n sends S to an equal-dimensional set in WithLp 2 (Rn(n)×ℝ). This is the identity on underlying pairs and equips the target with the Euclidean product norm.\\nClosed basis type:\\n∀ (n : ℕ) (S : Set (BasisObjects.Rn n × ℝ)),\\n  dimH (BasisObjects.euclideanLift n '' S) = dimH S\\nPrepared proof:\\nintro n S\\nexact YuiBasis.B005 (BasisObjects.Rn n × ℝ) (WithLp 2 (BasisObjects.Rn n × ℝ))\\n  (WithLp.prodContinuousLinearEquiv 2 ℝ (BasisObjects.Rn n) ℝ).symm S\", \"context\": \"Both factors are real normed spaces. prodContinuousLinearEquiv is a continuous linear equivalence from WithLp 2 (Rn(n)×ℝ) to the ordinary maximum-norm product. Its inverse has underlying map WithLp.toLp 2. ContinuousLinearEquiv.dimH_image, with scalar field ℝ, gives the displayed equality for every set and every natural n, without original H or measurability of S. No textbook result is assumed.\", \"qualification\": \"Prepared adapter from the explicitly supplied basis, retaining its exact permitted E statement. Its reduction is the inverse product continuous linear equivalence followed by the proved dimension-invariance theorem. It is not an imported class-3 project lemma. The source theorem and map identity have been read at the pinned commit; native basis checks remain separate from mathematical review.\", \"version_check\": \"2026-11-05: inspected frozen B011 type/proof and basis header, the actual pinned product equivalence and its inverse formula at ProdLp.lean:546–562, and the full ContinuousLinearEquiv.dimH_image source at HausdorffDimension.lean:412–420. The successful M015 probe confirms the latter full signature at commit 905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c. No mismatch of norm direction, map, domain, codomain or parameter range was found.\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/basis-registry.json\", \"sha256\": \"94cca4691b7a0d149adb23a24a120461be1a8e2eefb2f1daba11ffb1e138bfdd\"}, {\"path\": \"library/M015-0015/library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"d210bd0d451d17e10c417b536137d5ddabcc6b29a61b02bab71d7452a75cee15\"}], \"primary_evidence\": [{\"path\": \"inputs/basis-registry.json\", \"sha256\": \"94cca4691b7a0d149adb23a24a120461be1a8e2eefb2f1daba11ffb1e138bfdd\"}]}, \"type\": \"∀ (n : ℕ) (S : Set (BasisObjects.Rn n × ℝ)),\\n  dimH (BasisObjects.euclideanLift n '' S) = dimH S\"}], \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
{"id":["contract.json","/conclusion"],"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"contract.json\", \"pointer\": \"/conclusion\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": {\"statement\": \"dimH(G)=dimH(E)+1, where E has its original Euclidean metric and G is E×[0,1] identified with Euclidean Rn(n₀+1) as specified in the selected target; this holds for every natural n₀, including n₀=0. Both sides are finite, so the equality also holds for real Hausdorff dimensions.\", \"type\": \"dimH (G n E) = dimH E + 1 ∧ dimH E < ⊤ ∧ dimH (G n E) < ⊤\"}, \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
{"path": "nl02lean/references/proof-repair.md", "total_lines": 129, "start": 1, "end": 129, "start_column": 0, "end_column": 81, "requested_end": 250, "has_more": false, "next_start": null, "next_column": 0, "next_end": null, "text": "1: # Retained authors, immutable interfaces and proof repair\n2: \n3: This protocol is generic. No domain, historical node ID or particular notation\n4: activates a repair. Task names below describe responsibilities, not a new ID scheme.\n5: \n6: ## Preprocessing author and independent reviewer\n7: \n8: The controller verifies actual accepted NL0/A authority, then retains one author\n9: through optional shared extraction and interface construction. A no-change decision\n10: skips incremental review. A changed source gets actual producer/substitution review\n11: by the separate retained preprocessing reviewer. Located failures return to that\n12: same author; changed candidates return to that same reviewer. Operational faults\n13: pause rather than masquerading as mathematical revision requests.\n14: \n15: The reviewer next checks the complete typed contract and plan: every external E\n16: and full P including all hypotheses, quantifiers and domains; relevant definitions,\n17: structure fields, library parameter/index meanings, coercions and nonvacuity. Read\n18: the actual pinned definitions instead of guessing from notation. A well-typed\n19: encoding may still strengthen or weaken the source. The reviewer is independent\n20: of the author, while retaining its own earlier findings; disclose both facts.\n21: \n22: Only a complete actual passing semantic receipt opens freeze and proof dispatch.\n23: Freeze source, pins, complete endpoint types and the meaning-determining header.\n24: Save the handoff and close both preprocessing roles. An unchanged rejected proposal\n25: does not justify repeated reviews. This semantic authority is inherited at final\n26: machine acceptance only when the actual final artifact still matches it.\n27: \n28: ## Local proof work\n29: \n30: Each node has its own retained author. Ordinary compiler/API/tactic feedback stays\n31: with that author. A 180-second native timeout or heartbeat failure retains the\n32: candidate and supplies frozen ST020 advice in the failed tool result. Isolate the\n33: expensive application, repair and recheck the unchanged target. There is no total\n34: case, token, call, repair-round or turn allowance by default.\n35: \n36: `report_proof_obstruction` requests a diagnosis; it does not itself finish the\n37: node. The original author waits for this tool result, releases its active model\n38: slot, and retains its native conversation, checked helpers and drafts. A separate\n39: evidence-informed diagnostic uses the same bounded proof lane and an immutable\n40: snapshot of this node's source, interface and selected actual attempts. It does\n41: not wait for other nodes to complete or inspect their proof bodies.\n42: \n43: Ordinary proof errors remain local. A failed compile or resource limit does not\n44: establish a source defect. LOCAL_PROOF_REPAIR and UNRESOLVED return concrete\n45: feedback to the same author, which continues without an attempt/idle threshold.\n46: Rewording the same evidence reuses the stored diagnostic rather than sampling a\n47: new verdict. Healthy siblings are neither cancelled nor restarted.\n48: \n49: A source allegation must quote the current source, state its permitted\n50: hypotheses and claimed conclusion, locate a false implication or important\n51: missing mathematical justification, and explain why it is not merely a Lean\n52: implementation difficulty. Only a qualified diagnostic confirming such evidence\n53: can request the source-review boundary. Distinguish an incomplete source argument\n54: from a false theorem. Report the specific issue once; retain all unsuccessful\n55: attempts and diagnostic costs.\n56: \n57: The diagnostic cannot add premises or change frozen E/P and their meaning.\n58: Only a located, compared and qualified interface defect can hand the node to the\n59: existing interface-repair path. No fixed count of failed checks or idle replies\n60: retires an author. Progress counters measure activity, not proof completion.\n61: \n62: `INTERNAL_INTERFACE_REPAIR` returns a located T-statement/allowed-input defect to\n63: the preprocessing author and separate reviewer after current-round handoffs.\n64: Only steps may change: retain the entire public header, original H/E/P, source,\n65: pins, output map and public representations. Preserve the old frozen revision.\n66: Strict declaration/source-boundary checks precede an independent delta review\n67: of changed steps and affected consumers. Exact unchanged scopes inherit the\n68: bound parent receipt; a new actual receipt binds the new revision and review\n69: scope. Rejected candidates/findings stay with the same repair roles. Close the\n70: preprocessing roles at the existing successful freeze boundary. A closed role\n71: is restored from its own saved handoff in a disclosed fresh carrier; no vanished\n72: native conversation is claimed. Previously checked own bodies are candidates\n73: until current native checks accept them; changed packets cannot inherit old\n74: helper-check status. No sibling is forcibly closed for an obstruction.\n75: If a reviewed revision removes an old node, its retained repair handoff closes\n76: only at final workflow acceptance. Preserve that old obstruction rather than\n77: relabeling it as a successful local proof.\n78: \n79: A located protected public-contract defect or confirmed substantial source gap\n80: pauses with saved evidence for the user. Uncertain difficulty stays with the author. No automatic unfreeze, source rewrite,\n81: whole-workflow restart or new model/profile is authorized. Repeated identical\n82: obligation/evidence after a located advice handoff stays an explicit checkpoint;\n83: it is not a reason to resample a diagnostic or declare mathematical impossibility.\n84: \n85: ## Assembly and final machine acceptance\n86: \n87: Attempt mechanical assembly first. If it fails with an actual connection error,\n88: use the connection author under the immutable contract. Recheck the complete\n89: standalone file, all internal producer dependencies and actual kernel trust.\n90: No `sorry`, undeclared project premise or unproved substitute may remain.\n91: \n92: Normal final Audit B checks actual full E/P types by native definitional equality,\n93: exact frozen header/pins and bound semantic authority, plus complete proof closure.\n94: It calls no final semantic model. Its receipt says `FROZEN_INTERFACE_SEMANTICS_INHERITED`,\n95: not that a new independent final review took place. Source qualifications remain.\n96: \n97: Only a valid proof with genuine endpoint/meaning drift may open B-final. Missing\n98: compilation, changed provenance, missing dependencies or `sorry` cannot use this\n99: exception. Actual B-final fidelity PASS permits warning-qualified delivery and\n100: records the freeze-integrity defect. A nonpass pauses locally and asks the user:\n101: \n102: - `deliver-actual`: deliver the compiled statement and findings, explicitly **not**\n103:   completion of the original target.\n104: - `recheck`: perform the explicitly requested new detailed semantic review, keeping\n105:   earlier findings, costs and its actual review scope in the evidence.\n106: - `reset-translation`: release translation processes, remove translation-generated\n107:   source/native/temp files, retain original NL0/A and cost ledgers, and rerun only\n108:   translation. Report the freeze defect to developers.\n109: \n110: These are exact-incident `yui.py final-decision` controls displayed in the offline\n111: pause report. None is automatic. A stale incident, changed owner, changed artifact\n112: or unconfirmed owned-process cleanup blocks the action. User cancellation remains\n113: available. A user acceptance of files never grants fidelity or original-goal PASS.\n114: \n115: ## Evidence and lifetimes\n116: \n117: A submission is a durable handoff, followed by a confirmed native turn interrupt;\n118: its role waits without another provider request. The native thread is reused for\n119: that role's next assignment. Only the specified accepted handoff closes a role;\n120: a fault preserves the carrier, drafts and diagnostics. Controller-authenticated\n121: restoration of an interrupted dynamic-tool result is logged and hash-bound; unknown\n122: calls or altered history remain rejected.\n123: \n124: Reuse only exact checked contexts. Successful bodies copied between retained proof\n125: runs are candidates until current Lean checks accept them. Save rejected drafts,\n126: actual retries/waits and all reported usage. Selected proof rounds use bound\n127: `proof-selection.json`; retained earlier failures are not free or new successes.\n128: Missing usage or actual service tier remains unknown; cached/reasoning tokens are\n129: subsets. Owner monitoring is bounded read-only observation and supplies no hints."}

